Three bugs found auditing the macOS paths of nsiproxy.  All three are
present in wine master (11.14) as well, none has an upstream fix yet.

1. tcp.c / udp.c leak the IPv6 address scope table on every connection
   table enumeration.  get_ipv6_addr_scope_table() returns a malloc'd
   table that the caller owns; both enumerators free only their own
   endpoint/connection buffer.  nsiproxy runs inside the long lived
   winedevice.exe, and anything polling GetTcpTable/GetUdpTable (Steam
   and Chromium both do) leaks a little on every poll, without bound.

2. ipv4_forward_enumerate_all() checks whether a sockaddr runs past the
   end of the route message, logs an ERR, and then reads it anyway.  The
   check as written prevents nothing.  Bail out of the address loop
   instead, and clamp the copy into the fixed size sockaddr_in so a
   sa_len larger than the destination cannot smash the stack.

3. get_ipv6_addr_scope_table() dereferences ifa_addr without checking it
   for NULL.  getifaddrs(3) documents that ifa_addr may be NULL, and
   wine's own ip.c guards it in four other places (lines 841, 858, 1069
   and 1126 in master).  Only this one site does not.  Not observed
   firing on the test machine, where all 49 interfaces have an address,
   so this is a latent crash rather than one seen in practice.

diff --git a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c
index 32ba624..fe44706 100644
--- a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c
+++ b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c
@@ -1411,6 +1411,7 @@ static NTSTATUS ipv4_forward_enumerate_all( void *key_data, UINT key_size, void
                 {
                     ERR( "struct sockaddr extends beyond the route message, %p > %p\n",
                          addr_ptr + sa->sa_len, next + rtm->rtm_msglen );
+                    break;
                 }
 
                 if (sa->sa_len) addr_ptr += (sa->sa_len + sizeof(int)-1) & ~(sizeof(int)-1);
@@ -1427,7 +1428,7 @@ static NTSTATUS ipv4_forward_enumerate_all( void *key_data, UINT key_size, void
                      * 5 bytes (1 sa_len, 1 sa_family, 2 sa_port and 1 for the first
                      * byte of sin_addr). */
                     struct sockaddr_in sin = {0};
-                    memcpy( &sin, sa, sa->sa_len );
+                    memcpy( &sin, sa, min( sa->sa_len, sizeof(sin) ) );
                     addr = sin.sin_addr;
                     break;
                 }
diff --git a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/tcp.c b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/tcp.c
index 6b1bb9f..d7a0b63 100644
--- a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/tcp.c
+++ b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/tcp.c
@@ -239,7 +239,7 @@ struct ipv6_addr_scope *get_ipv6_addr_scope_table( unsigned int *size )
             struct sockaddr_in6 *sin6;
             struct ipv6_addr_scope *entry;
 
-            if (cur->ifa_addr->sa_family != AF_INET6) continue;
+            if (!cur->ifa_addr || cur->ifa_addr->sa_family != AF_INET6) continue;
 
             if (++num > table_size)
             {
@@ -386,6 +386,7 @@ static NTSTATUS tcp_conns_enumerate_all( UINT filter, struct nsi_tcp_conn_key *k
         }
     }
 
+    free( addr_scopes );
     free( connections );
     return STATUS_SUCCESS;
 }
diff --git a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/udp.c b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/udp.c
index 7bb93c9..0e24143 100644
--- a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/udp.c
+++ b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/udp.c
@@ -282,6 +282,7 @@ static NTSTATUS udp_endpoint_enumerate_all( void *key_data, UINT key_size, void
         }
     }
 
+    free( addr_scopes );
     free( endpoints );
     return STATUS_SUCCESS;
 }
